          "The most important strategic fact about HD-7 is the demographic gap between the Black community (23.92%) and the college-educated professional base (38%): these are distinct constituencies with overlapping but not identical concerns. The Black community, concentrated at roughly one-quarter of the district, faces specific barriers around homeownership, wealth-building, healthcare access, and economic mobility that the college-educated professional majority may not experience or prioritize in the same way. The renter rate at 41.3% — nearly five percentage points above the Black population percentage — signals that renters are drawn from across racial lines and face the shared constraint of housing instability regardless of other characteristics. A 70% Democratic result in a diverse district reflects successful alignment on education investment, healthcare access, and whether government is delivering competent service, but it does not reflect overwhelming enthusiasm or guaranteed durability. The incumbent or winning challenger is the one who articulates and delivers on specific answers to the Black community's economic security concerns, to renters' housing stability and affordability constraints, and to the college-educated professional base's expectations for government competence — not the one who assumes the 70% margin is self-sustaining."
