          "The 42% Republican opposition in an affluent, educated district is neither marginal nor ideologically alien — it represents college-educated professionals and homeowners who have concluded that government is inefficiently run, taxes are unjustifiably high, or state policy should prioritize business competitiveness and reduce regulatory burden. This opposition is sophisticated, income-secure, and capable of mobilizing around specific governance failures or tax-efficiency arguments. The 22.4% renter rate — roughly one in five households — represents the strategic vulnerability in any Democratic-held seat claiming to represent affluent homeowners: renters in HD-8 face housing costs, economic precarity, and different governance priorities than homeowners, and a candidate who positions entirely around homeowner concerns (property tax, school quality, infrastructure for owner-occupied neighborhoods) will lose contact with the renter constituency that Moore successfully engaged. Holding or winning HD-8 requires simultaneously serving the affluent homeowner majority with fiscal discipline and school-quality credentials, engaging the college-educated professional workforce on economic opportunity and diversity expectations, and meeting renters where they live on housing costs and economic security — a balancing act that the 58-42 margin suggests the incumbent has navigated, and that any challenger would need to disrupt with precision targeting rather than broad ideological appeals."
