          { name: "College-Educated Suburban Constituency & Quality-of-Life Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 47.5% college attainment and 38.9 median age, HD-9 is a suburban, educated, professional constituency that evaluates candidates on governance competence, infrastructure quality, and whether their children have access to excellent schools and economic opportunity. This demographic cohort is less responsive to partisan abstraction and more responsive to concrete delivery on schools, roads, utilities, and professional-class quality of life. The 47% Democratic share in a high-income district signals that some college-educated households are persuadable on the basis of local governance track record rather than partisan loyalty—a candidate who can credibly demonstrate competent delivery on infrastructure and education is building a durable coalition." },
          { name: "Understanding the 47% Democratic Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "A 53-47 margin in a district with $141,298 income, 94.5% homeownership, and 47.5% college attainment is narrower than the district's demographic profile would predict—most similar districts produce 60%+ Republican results. The 47% opposition among affluent, college-educated homeowners suggests either specific governance failures, tax/fiscal disagreement with the incumbent, or suburban swing-voter persuadability on education and infrastructure quality. Identifying whether the opposition is concentrated among younger professionals, college-educated women, or households frustrated with specific legislative outcomes is essential to understanding whether the 53% represents a durable majority or a vulnerable plurality." },
          { name: "Treating a 53% Margin as Comfortable in an Affluent, Persuadable District", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district this wealthy and this educated, a 53-47 result is not a safe majority—it is a genuinely competitive outcome that reflects meaningful persuasion of high-income, college-educated voters away from the Republican baseline. These households have the political sophistication and financial resources to organize alternative campaigns if they conclude incumbent performance is inadequate. Any candidate who campaigns a 53% hold as though it represents a secure base rather than actively engaging the 47% opposition is underestimating the volatility of an educated, affluent constituency that evaluates on governance record." },
          { name: "Partisan Ideology Over Suburban Governance Priorities", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-9's college-educated, professional-class constituency is motivated primarily by effective local governance, education quality, and infrastructure competence—not abstract partisan positioning. A candidate who emphasizes culture-war messaging, partisan identity, or ideology over concrete delivery on schools, roads, and professional-class quality of life will struggle to hold the attention of households earning $141k+ whose primary concern is whether their children have access to excellent schools and their neighborhood maintains its character. The 47% opposition in this demographic profile suggests that some affluent voters are actively open to supporting candidates who lead with governance competence rather than partisan branding." }
