          { name: "Treating the 37% Republican Base as Permanently Consolidated", tag: "avoid", why: "A 63-37 Democratic margin in a district at $78,112 median income with 76.7% homeownership suggests that white homeowners are not reflexively Republican in HD-19 — they are evaluating on economic governance and whether the current representative is delivering for middle-class stability. Any Democratic candidate who campaigns as though the 37% Republican opposition is a fixed floor of unpersuadable voters misses the actual strategic fact: the homeowner majority chose Democratic governance, which means they chose it on merit. If a Republican challenger offers a credible case that middle-class economic security would improve under different governance, this margin is vulnerable." },
          { name: "Ignoring Rural or Agricultural Economy Components", tag: "avoid", why: "While HD-19's profile is predominantly suburban or small-town middle-class, the 23.3% renter rate and $78,112 median income do not preclude rural constituencies or agricultural workers whose economic concerns differ from the homeowner majority. A candidate who frames the district solely as a urban-suburban middle-class story and ignores any agricultural, ranching, or rural service-delivery dimensions risks alienating a constituency that may be small but is politically engaged and responsive to candidates who understand their specific economic pressures." }
