          { name: "College-Educated, Professional-Class Constituency Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 64% college attainment — among the highest in Delaware — HD-22 is a highly educated, professional-class district where voters evaluate candidates on substantive policy detail and intellectual credibility. This constituency is sensitive to governance competence, fiscal responsibility, and evidence-based policymaking; generic talking points are less persuasive here than concrete legislative proposals grounded in data. Candidates who lead with education, healthcare access, and workforce development — framed around the district's highly skilled workforce and their economic interests — will resonate more effectively than those offering simplified partisan narratives." },
          { name: "The 45% Democratic Opposition in a Wealthy District", tag: "careful", why: "A 55-45 Republican result in a $161K+ income, 90% homeowner, 64% college-educated district is not a secure majority — it represents a genuinely competitive constituency where 45% of affluent, educated voters actively chose the Democratic candidate. This opposition is concentrated among the district's college-educated professionals, many of whom likely work in healthcare, education, or other sectors where state policy directly affects their employment and economic interests. Understanding specifically why affluent, educated homeowners in this district are split nearly evenly between parties is more important than assuming the 55% Republican result is stable; it likely reflects divisions on social policy, governance style, and the state's direction that transcend traditional economic conservatism." },
          { name: "Treating Affluent Voters as a Monolithic Conservative Base", tag: "avoid", why: "The 55-45 margin in this wealthy, highly educated district is a warning against assuming that income and homeownership automatically produce reliable Republican support. Affluent, college-educated voters — particularly in Northern Delaware's professional-class suburbs — evaluate on specific governance competence, education quality, healthcare policy, and community investment. Any candidate who campaigns this district as though wealth alone guarantees party loyalty will miss the actual swing constituency: educated professionals concerned about state fiscal management, education funding, and whether government is delivering quality services to their community." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 9.9% Renter Minority", tag: "avoid", why: "While renters represent only 9.9% of HD-22, they are present — likely concentrated in multifamily or service-sector housing within the broader affluent geography — and any candidate who frames the district exclusively around homeowner concerns will miss this constituency and signal that their governance is premised on excluding neighbors who don't own property. In a district this educated and civic-minded, visible exclusion of a minority population from the candidate's framing is both a values signal and a political vulnerability; inclusive language and genuine engagement with working families in the district is more strategically sound than homeowner-only positioning." }
