          { name: "Young, Renter-Heavy Urban or Town-Center Population", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 29.6-year median age and 36.8% renter rate are among the youngest and most renter-heavy in Oklahoma's legislative districts, signaling a constituency of young professionals, early-career workers, and graduate students for whom housing affordability, student debt, and access to good jobs are direct economic concerns. This demographic profile — young, educated, and renting rather than owning — typically correlates with openness to new ideas, skepticism of purely traditional governance approaches, and prioritization of economic opportunity over cultural conservatism. In a district this young and this educated, a candidate who addresses housing affordability, student debt relief, and the cost of living for young professionals speaks directly to the material constraints shaping daily decisions for a large portion of the constituency." },
          { name: "Unopposed Status & Implicit Vulnerability", tag: "careful", why: "Gorman won unopposed in 2024, which in a 100%-0% partisan result masks a critical strategic reality: a district this affluent, this educated, and this young does not run 100%-0% because the electorate is united, but because no one mounted a credible challenge. At $105K median income and 60% college attainment, HD-23 contains the demographic profile most likely to split tickets and reward competence over party loyalty if presented with a credible alternative. The unopposed status is a gift that obscures the absence of demonstrated voter enthusiasm and the latent vulnerability that comes with never having to defend the seat against genuine opposition." },
          { name: "Treating Educational Investment & Professional Development as 'Special Interests'", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 60% of adults hold college degrees and median income is $105K, framing education funding, student loan policy, or professional licensing as special-interest positions rather than as core economic competitiveness issues will be perceived as fundamentally disconnected from the lived experience of the dominant constituency. A significant portion of HD-23's voters are either currently employed in professional sectors or have invested heavily in the credentials and education required to access them; treating these as marginal concerns signals a candidate who does not understand who actually lives in the district." },
          { name: "Taking Young, Educated Voters for Granted as a Partisan Base", tag: "avoid", why: "The combination of youth (29.6 median age), education (60.2% college-attainment), and renter status (36.8%) creates a constituency that is socially liberal and fiscally pragmatic — not reliably partisan in either direction if the incumbent is not actively delivering on economic concerns. Young professionals in high-income districts prioritize their own economic mobility, housing affordability, and the practical delivery of services; they are not motivated primarily by cultural or traditional partisan loyalty. An incumbent who campaigns this seat as if it is safely held by partisan alignment, rather than by continuous demonstration of economic competence and responsiveness to young professionals' material concerns, is taking unacknowledged risk." }
