          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "Carson's unopposed 2024 victory is not a signal of durable strength; it reflects current partisan alignment in a district with 31% Black residents, 28.2% renters, and a median income of $67,932 — demographics that suggest economic vulnerability and a constituency that will reward or punish based on material responsiveness. Unopposed status typically emerges when potential challengers perceive the incumbent as entrenched, not necessarily when the constituency is satisfied. Any representative who treats unopposed victory as permission to reduce constituent engagement, defer on service delivery issues, or take demographic communities for granted is creating the opening that a credible challenger will exploit." },
          { name: "Assuming Partisan Alignment Substitutes for Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "A district this economically pressured — 17.8% college attainment, 28.2% renters, $67,932 median income — evaluates elected officials on whether they deliver material improvements in schools, public safety, utility costs, and economic opportunity, not on partisan positioning. The unopposed victory should not be misread as ideological alignment; it reflects demographic concentration and current candidate scarcity, not constituency satisfaction. Any representative who leads with partisan rhetoric rather than concrete responses to housing affordability, education quality, and working-family economic security is vulnerable the moment a credible challenger emerges offering specific solutions to these material concerns." }
