          { name: "Treating the District as Homogeneous", tag: "avoid", why: "The district's demographic composition — 46.39% Black, 33.16% white, 10.13% Hispanic, 3.44% Asian, plus ~7% other categories — requires granular engagement with each community's distinct economic and social concerns. Any candidate whose messaging treats the district as a monolithic bloc rather than as distinct communities with overlapping but separate needs will miss the opportunity to build the layered coalition that sustained an unopposed seat requires." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Partisan Loyalty Over Material Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "A district where nearly half the residents are renters, median income is $58,862, and college attainment is only 30.4% is a constituency that evaluates primarily on whether government delivers material improvements to their economic and housing security — not on party label. Any candidate who assumes the unopposed Democratic result reflects deep partisan attachment rather than constituent satisfaction is ignoring the economic realities that drive actual voting behavior in working-class districts." }
