          "The district's electoral safety is contingent on sustained delivery to the Black community and the renter majority that overlaps significantly with it. At 51.4% renters with $58,862 median income, housing costs consume a disproportionate share of household budgets, and the pathway from renter status to ownership is economically blocked for many constituents — creating a material governance agenda around tenant protections, housing affordability, and the connection between stable housing and economic mobility. The Hispanic (10.13%), Asian (3.44%), and white (33.16%) communities add distinct economic and social dimensions that require specific engagement beyond the dominant Black plurality. Any representative who consolidates the Black vote through authentic leadership on employment, education, and housing while simultaneously engaging the renter majority and other communities on material concerns has built a coalition that can sustain an unopposed seat; one who defaults to partisan loyalty or generic messaging while material economic concerns go unaddressed will find the next election cycle produces a credible challenger."
