          { name: "Renter-Majority Constituency & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 54.5%, renters constitute the district's majority — an unusually high concentration that signals economic precarity and geographic mobility among the constituency. Combined with a median age of 32.2 and median household income of $64,332, this is a young, working-class community where housing costs consume a disproportionate share of income and eviction risk is a material economic threat. Housing policy — rent stabilization, tenant protections, affordable housing development, and zoning reform — is not an abstract equity issue here; it is the primary economic security concern for a majority of constituents. A representative who treats housing affordability as a core governance priority rather than a secondary equity concern is aligned with the district's demographic reality." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Representation & Labor Market Access", tag: "careful", why: "At 13.08%, the Hispanic community is small enough that it can become invisible in a district dominated by the Black-white narrative, yet large enough that its economic and civic concerns are material to district outcomes. With only 26.1% college attainment district-wide and a median income of $64,332, the Hispanic community's primary concerns are likely labor market access, workplace protections, and educational opportunity for younger generations. Neglecting this community's specific needs — treating it as a secondary minority constituency rather than an active constituent base — is both a governance failure and a latent electoral vulnerability, particularly if Hispanic voters perceive that their concerns are being subordinated to other priorities." },
          { name: "Treating 62% as an Insurmountable Democratic Advantage", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62-38 margin is a solid Democratic hold, not a permanent guarantee — particularly in a district where 38% of voters chose the Republican candidate and economic stress is visible in the income and renter-rate numbers. If Democratic messaging becomes distant from the material economic concerns of the renter-majority, working-class constituency — housing costs, healthcare access, wage stagnation — the opposition has an opening to argue that the incumbent is not delivering for working people. A Democratic representative who takes the 62% for granted and fails to demonstrate concrete progress on the issues that matter most to renters and lower-income households risks mobilizing opposition that the 62% margin cannot absorb." },
          { name: "Positioning Education Access as Secondary to Other Priorities", tag: "avoid", why: "With only 26.1% college attainment and a median age of 32.2, HD-32 is a young district where educational pathway and workforce opportunity are crucial to economic mobility for the next decade. If a representative's agenda treats K-12 funding, vocational training, and post-secondary access as secondary issues subordinated to other priorities, the district's young adults — particularly in the Black and Hispanic communities — have rational grounds to view the incumbent as not invested in their opportunity. Education is not a boutique issue here; it is the material difference between economic mobility and stagnation for a constituency with limited inherited wealth and high economic precarity." }
