          { name: "Racial & Ethnic Diversity in an Affluent Urban Setting", tag: "careful", why: "At 68.99% white, 15.73% Black, 6.15% Asian, and 5.07% Hispanic, plus a remaining ~4% gap likely multiracial, SD-5 is a racially and ethnically diverse affluent urban district — not a white homeowner enclave. The 66% Democratic margin suggests broad coalition alignment, but the different economic interests and civic priorities of these communities require active, differentiated engagement. Black and Asian professional communities in particular are significant presences in San Diego's professional labor market whose economic advancement concerns, healthcare access equity, and civic leadership opportunities should be specifically addressed rather than absorbed into generic 'diversity' framing." },
          { name: "Treating 66% as a Durable Democratic Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66-34 result in California is a strong Democratic margin, but in an affluent, highly educated urban district where homeownership is 72.4% and income is six figures above state median, this constituency is capable of shifting if it concludes the incumbent is not fiscally responsible or is mismanaging urban livability. Treating 66% as a safe baseline — rather than actively serving the economic security concerns of affluent professionals, the education agenda of college-educated voters, and the civic expectations of a racially diverse urban community — is the fastest path to erosion against a credible challenger." },
          { name: "Partisan Culture War Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "SD-5's 47.1% college attainment rate and $100,446 income indicate a constituency that evaluates on material governance — education policy, fiscal management, urban livability, and economic opportunity — not cultural grievance. Any candidate whose primary message revolves around partisan cultural conflict, rather than specific solutions to the cost of living, housing affordability, or educational excellence, is misreading this district's actual decision-making framework and ceding attention to a challenger who speaks to their real concerns." }
