          { name: "Treating 52% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52-48 result is a working plurality in a competitive district, not a durable majority. Any incumbent or challenger who frames this seat as 'held' or assumes the current voter coalition will remain stable without active engagement is strategically misreading the fundamentals. In a district where the Black population at 29% is larger than the white population at 56.5%, where median age is 37.5 and renter rate is 14.8%, demographic change and turnout variation are ongoing factors that can shift a 4-point margin in either direction with relatively modest changes in candidate performance or voter mobilization." },
          { name: "Racial Resentment Framing or Dog-Whistle Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where Black constituents at 29.33% represent a larger share of the population than any other single group, and where the Latino population at 8.38% and Asian population at 2.26% together add another ~10.5%, any candidate whose positioning relies on racial resentment framing, immigrant scapegoating, or coded 'law and order' messaging will find that framing actively hostile to the district's actual demographic reality and material interests. The district's majority is not a white working-class community; it is a diverse, economically mobile constituency where racial justice and economic opportunity are interconnected concerns that resonate across all demographic groups." }
